// Documented open front-end defect (book-verification 2026-09-19, repros r_not / r_not2).
// Spec §2.2 lists `not` among `unary_operator` keywords ("Unary operators: `not`, `bnot`
// (right associative)"), §3.3.3 shows `not p` and `not (p and q)` as ordinary unary-negation
// expressions, and §3.3.5 uses it in guards (`n: int64 if not is_even(n) -> ...`). It is an
// operator, not a function -- confirmed before filing this trial.
//
// The parser/type-checker do not implement `not` as a general unary prefix operator; only the
// guard-clause use works. Every other position tried fails, each a different way:
//
//   1. As a call argument -- `print_bool(not true)` -- E1069 ParseError: "function 'main' has
//      no parsable body; an expression inside it could not be parsed (for example a call whose
//      function name is a keyword such as link)" (a generic message; `not`, not `link`, is the
//      keyword that broke parsing here).
//   2. As a case scrutinee -- `case not true of { true -> 1; false -> 0 }` (r_not) -- E2005
//      "cannot infer type of case scrutinee".
//   3. As a let-binding's expression (filed below, r_not2) -- E2002 "undefined identifier: not":
//      the type checker falls through to treating `not` as an unresolved plain identifier.
//
// Expected: compiles; `not true` is `false`; prints "false", exit 0.
//   false
//   0
// Observed: E2002 at the `<-` binding's right-hand side.

fn main() -> int64 {
    sequence proc[device_io]
        flipped: boolean <- not true;
        _: atom <- print_bool(flipped);
        println("")
    produces
        pure 0
    end
}
